CH = dict(num=12, tag="Physics", title="Magnetic Effects of Electric Current", theme="Field lines, field due to current, force on a conductor, motor, induction, generator, household circuits",
intro="A compass needle swings when a current-carrying wire is nearby: electricity and magnetism are linked. This link powers electric motors in fans and mixers, generators in power stations, transformers on poles and the circuits inside our homes. The chapter studies magnetic fields, the field produced by currents, the force on a current in a field, and electromagnetic induction.",
big_questions=["How does an electric current produce a magnetic field?", "How does a magnetic field exert force and run a motor?", "How is electric current produced by changing magnetic fields?"],
blocks=[
("h2", "12.1 Magnetic Field and Field Lines"),
("p", "A magnet has two poles, north-seeking and south-seeking; like poles repel, unlike poles attract. The region around a magnet in which its force can be felt is the **magnetic field**. Field lines are traced with iron filings or a compass."),
("ul", ["Field lines emerge from the **north pole** and merge at the **south pole** outside the magnet; inside they go from south to north, forming **closed curves**.", "The **closeness** of lines shows the strength of the field; field lines never **intersect**, since the compass can point in only one direction at a place."]),
("h2", "12.2 Magnetic Field due to a Current"),
("p", "**Oersted (1820)** found that a compass needle near a wire deflects when current flows, and the deflection reverses when the current reverses."),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Right-hand thumb rule:** hold the straight conductor in the right hand with the thumb along the current; the fingers curl in the direction of the **magnetic field** lines."),
("table", ["Conductor", "Field pattern", "Dependence"], [["Straight wire", "Concentric circles around the wire", "Stronger with larger current and nearer the wire"], ["Circular loop", "Circles near the wire become larger and larger farther away; straight at the centre", "Stronger with more turns, larger current, smaller radius"], ["**Solenoid** (long coil)", "Like a bar magnet; **uniform** field inside, parallel straight lines", "Stronger with more turns and larger current; soft iron core makes an **electromagnet**"]]),
("ul", ["A solenoid has a north and south pole like a bar magnet; one end behaves as north.", "A **permanent magnet** is made of steel, an **electromagnet** of soft iron, whose magnetism disappears when current stops."]),
("h2", "12.3 Force on a Current-carrying Conductor"),
("p", "A current-carrying rod in a magnetic field experiences a force; **Andr&eacute;-Marie Amp&egrave;re** suggested that a magnet exerts an equal and opposite force on the conductor. The force is **maximum** when the current is at **right angles** to the field, and its direction reverses if the current or the field direction is reversed."),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Fleming's left-hand rule:** stretch the **forefinger, middle finger and thumb** of the left hand at right angles. If the forefinger points in the direction of the **field** and the middle finger in the direction of the **current**, the thumb gives the direction of the **force** (motion)."),
("h3", "Electric motor"),
("p", "A motor converts **electrical energy to mechanical energy**. A rectangular coil (armature) is between the poles of a magnet; the two sides carry current in opposite directions, so they experience opposite forces and the coil rotates. A **split-ring commutator** reverses the current every half turn, so the coil keeps turning in the same direction. Brushes link the commutator with the battery. Motors drive fans, washing machines, mixers, pumps and many other devices; strength increases with a stronger magnet, more turns, more current and soft iron core."),
("h2", "12.4 Electromagnetic Induction"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Electromagnetic induction** is the production of an induced potential difference (and current) in a coil when the magnetic field around it **changes** (Faraday, 1831). It is produced by moving a magnet relative to a coil, or by changing the current in a nearby coil."),
("ul", ["A galvanometer needle deflects only while the magnet is moving; deflection is opposite when the magnet is withdrawn.", "**Fleming's right-hand rule:** forefinger = field, thumb = motion of conductor, middle finger = direction of **induced current**."]),
("h3", "Electric generator"),
("p", "A generator converts **mechanical energy into electrical energy**. A coil rotating in a magnetic field cuts field lines, and an induced current flows; the current changes direction after every half rotation, giving **alternating current (AC)**. With **slip rings** the output is AC; with a **split-ring commutator** it is **direct current (DC)**."),
("table", ["Feature", "AC", "DC"], [["Direction", "Reverses periodically; in India every 1/100 s (frequency **50 Hz**)", "Fixed direction (cell, battery)"], ["Source", "Power stations, generators", "Cells, batteries, solar cells"], ["Advantage", "**Transmitted over long distances** with little loss", "Used in electronics"]]),
("h2", "12.5 Domestic Electric Circuits"),
("ul", ["Supply from the mains reaches homes through a **live wire (red, positive)** and **neutral wire (black)** with a potential difference of 220 V; the **earth wire (green)** is connected to a metal plate deep in the earth.", "The **earth wire** gives a low-resistance path for leakage current to the ground, so the body of the appliance (kettle, refrigerator) stays at earth potential and the user avoids shock.", "**Short circuit:** live and neutral touch, current becomes very large; **overloading:** too many appliances on one socket, drawing excess current. A **fuse** or an MCB protects against both.", "Circuits for each room are **parallel**; a 15 A line is used for heavy appliances (geyser, AC), 5 A for bulbs and fans."]),
("h2", "12.6 Worked Examples"),
("p", "**Direction of force.** Current flows from east to west in a wire placed in a vertical field pointing downward. The force is perpendicular to both, so it acts along the north-south line; applying the left-hand rule (F = I L x B) gives **south**."),
("p", "**Choosing the rule.** Motor: current and field known, find force &rarr; **left-hand rule**. Generator: motion and field known, find induced current &rarr; **right-hand rule**."),
("p", "**Why does a coil rotate continuously in a motor?** The commutator reverses the current every half turn, so the force on each side keeps the same turning sense."),
("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
("qa", [("What did Oersted observe?", "Current in a wire deflects a nearby compass needle, so currents produce magnetic fields."), ("State Fleming's left-hand rule.", "Forefinger: field; middle finger: current; thumb: force."), ("Why do magnetic field lines never cross?", "Two tangents at a point would give two field directions, which is impossible."), ("What is the function of the commutator?", "Reverses the current every half rotation in the motor."), ("Name two safety devices in a home circuit.", "Fuse and earth wire (also MCB)."), ("What is the frequency of AC in India?", "50 Hz."), ("What is the role of the soft iron core in an electromagnet?", "It becomes strongly magnetised and strengthens the field."), ("What is the difference between AC and DC generators?", "AC uses slip rings; DC uses a split-ring commutator.")]),
],
takeaways=["Current produces a magnetic field; the **right-hand thumb rule** gives its direction.", "A **solenoid** carrying current behaves like a bar magnet; iron core gives an **electromagnet**.", "**Force on conductor:** Fleming's **left-hand rule**; the **motor** converts electrical to mechanical energy.", "**Induction:** changing field produces current; **right-hand rule**; generator converts mechanical to electrical energy.", "Home wiring: **live, neutral, earth**; fuse prevents overloading."])
